  <h2 id="manuscript">Manuscript (DOCX, PDF)</h2>
  <p>Standard manuscript format for sending to agents, editors and publishers.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>US Letter, 1″ margins, Courier 12 pt, double-spaced, first lines indented.</li>
    <li>Running header on every page of the text: <em>Author / TITLE / page</em>.</li>
    <li>Each chapter starts on a new page with its heading centred (or runs on, with Chapter Start set to Continuous). Parts get a page of their own.</li>
    <li>Front matter from the <strong>Manuscript Format</strong> folder (a title page with your word count by default).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="paperback">Paperback and Proof PDF</h2>
  <p>Print-ready interiors for print-on-demand services such as Amazon KDP and IngramSpark.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Format</th><th>Trim size</th><th>Use it for</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Paperback 5.06″ × 7.81″</td><td>5.06 × 7.81 in (mass-market)</td><td>Uploading the interior</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Paperback 6″ × 9″</td><td>6 × 9 in (trade)</td><td>Uploading the interior</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Proof PDF 5.06″ × 7.81″ / 6″ × 9″</td><td>Same, plus bleed and crop marks</td><td>Checking the layout yourself, or at a print shop</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <ul>
    <li>Times 11 pt, justified, with a running header: page number and author on even pages, title and page number on odd pages.</li>
    <li>The first paragraph after a heading or scene break isn't indented, as in printed books.</li>
    <li>Front matter from the <strong>Paperback</strong> folder: title page, copyright, disclaimer, dedication and a blank page.</li>
    <li>Proof PDFs carry a faint <em>PROOF</em> watermark and crop marks on every page. Don't upload them as your final interior.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="epub">EPUB e-book</h2>
  <p>A standard EPUB 3 e-book for Apple Books, Kobo, Google Play Books and Barnes &amp; Noble, and for Amazon KDP, which accepts EPUB uploads. To read it on a Kindle yourself, convert it with the free Calibre app.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Each chapter is its own section, listed in the e-book's table of contents.</li>
    <li>Front matter from the <strong>Ebook</strong> folder comes first and back matter last; neither is listed in the table of contents.</li>
    <li>Text formatting, colours and tables are kept. Images are not included in EPUB files yet.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="scripts">Screenplay, TV and documentary</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Screenplay (PDF):</strong> US Letter, Courier 12 pt, 1.5″ left margin. Headings become scene headings in capitals; short lines in capitals become character names (or transitions, if they end in a colon, like <code>CUT TO:</code>); the lines after a character name become dialogue; lines in brackets become parentheticals; everything else is action. Front matter comes from the <strong>Screenplay</strong> folder.</li>
    <li><strong>Screenplay (.fountain):</strong> the same script as a Fountain plain-text file, which screenwriting apps such as Final Draft, Highland and Fade In can open.</li>
    <li><strong>Documentary Script (PDF / Fountain):</strong> documentary format with voice-over (V/O) and on-camera cues. The PDF uses the <strong>TV Script</strong> front matter folder.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Use the <em>Screenplay Scene</em> or <em>TV Scene</em> template in the Templates section for a page that's already laid out the way the export expects.</p>

  <h2 id="plays">Stage plays</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Stage Play (PDF):</strong> US format (Samuel French / Dramatists Play Service). Act headings in words (“Act One”), stage directions in italics and brackets.</li>
    <li><strong>Stage Play UK (PDF):</strong> RSC / BBC style. Act headings in Roman numerals (“ACT I”), stage directions in italics without brackets.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Both are US Letter, Courier 12 pt, with front matter from the <strong>Manuscript Format</strong> folder.</p>

  <h2 id="academic">Academic papers and essays</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Format</th><th>What it sets up</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Academic Paper — APA 7th (DOCX, PDF)</td><td>Times New Roman 12 pt, double-spaced, 1″ margins, title page, abstract, running head, <em>References</em>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Academic Paper — MLA 9th (DOCX, PDF)</td><td>Times New Roman 12 pt, double-spaced, 1″ margins, <em>Surname page</em> header, <em>Works Cited</em>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Essay — Chicago 17th (DOCX, PDF)</td><td>Times New Roman 12 pt, double-spaced, title page, page numbers from page 2. Notes-bibliography style (footnotes and a <em>Bibliography</em>) by default; author-date style puts citations in brackets and ends with <em>References</em>.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>These formats use the style guide's own title page, not your front matter folders. You can adjust fonts, margins, labels and other settings in <a href="/docs/preferences#academic">Preferences → Academic</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="childrens">Children's books</h2>
  <p>Picture-book PDFs at <strong>8.5″ × 8.5″</strong> (square), <strong>8.5″ × 11″</strong> (portrait) or <strong>11″ × 8.5″</strong> (landscape).</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Each page document in your manuscript becomes one page of the book.</li>
    <li>The first image on a page fills its part of the page out to the bleed (0.125″). The text stays inside a 0.25″ safe zone, so nothing important gets trimmed.</li>
    <li>The text size adjusts to how much is on the page, from 18 pt for a line or two down to 10 pt for long passages.</li>
    <li>Choose whether the picture goes above or below the text for each page in the Inspector, under <strong>Image Position</strong>.</li>
    <li>Front matter comes from the <strong>Paperback</strong> folder, kept inside the safe zone.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="print">Print</h2>
  <p>The <strong>Print</strong> option in the Compile dialog, and <span class="menu">File → Print…</span> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>P</kbd>), open your computer's print dialog for what's on screen. <span class="menu">File → Print…</span> also works after the trial ends.</p>

  <h2 id="footnotes">Footnotes and images</h2>
  <p><strong>Footnotes</strong> are numbered afresh in every export, through the whole book in reading order, so deleting or moving one never leaves a gap.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Manuscript DOCX</strong> and the <strong>APA, MLA and Chicago</strong> DOCX exports make real Word footnotes.</li>
    <li><strong>Manuscript PDF, Paperback, Proof and EPUB</strong> put a number like [3] where each footnote is, and the notes in a numbered <em>Notes</em> list at the end of each chapter. The academic PDFs collect them on a Notes page.</li>
    <li>Scripts, plays and children's books don't use footnotes.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>Images</strong> are included in Manuscript DOCX and children's books, but not in EPUB.</p>
</DocsLayout>
